---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="About"
  description="Martin Sparks is a software engineering manager based in Scotland, with a background in full-stack engineering and a habit of running long distances."
>
  <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6 py-16">
    <h1 class="font-sans text-3xl font-bold mb-10" style="color: var(--color-text);">About me</h1>

    <div class="prose max-w-none" style="color: var(--color-text);">
      <p>
        Hi — I'm Martin. I'm a software engineering manager based in Scotland, currently at
        <a href="https://www.insights.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Insights</a>,
        where I lead a multi-disciplinary team of engineers, QA, and design. My background is in
        full-stack engineering — I spent several years writing React and TypeScript in the trenches
        before moving into a leadership role — and I try to keep enough technical depth to be useful
        to the people I work with.
      </p>

      <p>
        The path here wasn't entirely linear. I came to software through
        <a href="https://codeclan.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CodeClan</a>,
        an intensive 16-week bootcamp in Edinburgh, after a stint in technical delivery management
        for the Scottish Government. Before writing code professionally I was implementing a
        £1 million telephony platform into Registers of Scotland — managing suppliers, stakeholders,
        and the particular chaos of making technology work inside a public body. It turns out that
        experience translates well to engineering leadership: the job is still mostly about
        communication, priorities, and keeping things moving.
      </p>

      <p>
        At Insights I lead a team of ten engineers and own the technical roadmap. In the past year
        that's meant a large-scale migration off a legacy platform, establishing a community of
        practice, and building the kind of agile culture where people actually want to show up to
        retrospectives. The engineering side — React, TypeScript, Java — I still find genuinely
        interesting, even when I'm spending most of my time in Jira rather than a code editor.
      </p>

      <h2>Outside of work</h2>

      <p>
        I run. More than is probably sensible. Early mornings, long distances, often in the dark —
        there's a post about that somewhere on this blog. It's the best way I've found to think
        through hard problems and, occasionally, to stop thinking about them entirely.
      </p>

      <p>
        I'm also building a jigsaw puzzle game as a side project — Phaser 3, TypeScript, targeting
        iOS and Android via Capacitor. It's more technically interesting than it sounds. I'll be
        writing about it here as it takes shape.
      </p>

      <h2>This site</h2>

      <p>
        A place to think out loud. I write about engineering — decisions I've made, tools I'm using,
        things that went wrong in instructive ways. I write about running. And I write about building
        things in public, which means being honest about the dead ends as well as the progress.
      </p>

      <p>
        There's an <a href="/rss.xml">RSS feed</a> if you'd like to follow along.
      </p>

      <h2>Get in touch</h2>

      <p>
        Email is best:
        <a href="mailto:martin.sparks82@gmail.com">martin.sparks82@gmail.com</a>.
        I'm also on
        <a href="https://linkedin.com/in/martin-sparks" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">LinkedIn</a>,
        <a href="https://github.com/Martin-Sparks" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub</a>,
        and <a href="https://www.strava.com/athletes/martinsparks" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Strava</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
